\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The same $H_0$ diagram (DTM filtration, $k=5$ -- a plain Rips filtration gives every point birth $0$, so the birth axis this figure calibrates would be degenerate) -- the most feature-rich diagram among the non-outlier bulk (own max persistence at or below the 60th percentile), drawn from $2000$ clouds sampled from the production Thomas design distribution (Section~\ref {sec:simulator}) -- rendered under the naive pooled min/max bound (left) and the per-diagram coverage-quantile bound of $q=0.95$, $\alpha =1.05$ (right). Top row: the diagram's own points against the calibrated birth-persistence box, with the dashed square marking the region zoomed into below; the naive box is stretched by a single high-offspring outlier diagram elsewhere in the population, not shown. Bottom row: the same physical region of birth-persistence space, rendered at each calibration's native pixel density -- a $7\times 7$ block of mostly-fused pixels under the naive bound against a $16\times 16$ grid that keeps the secondary peak resolved under the quantile bound, since the same $64\times 64$ grid is stretched over a wider range.}}{14}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:calibration_comparison}{{3}{14}{The same $H_0$ diagram (DTM filtration, $k=5$ -- a plain Rips filtration gives every point birth $0$, so the birth axis this figure calibrates would be degenerate) -- the most feature-rich diagram among the non-outlier bulk (own max persistence at or below the 60th percentile), drawn from $2000$ clouds sampled from the production Thomas design distribution (Section~\ref {sec:simulator}) -- rendered under the naive pooled min/max bound (left) and the per-diagram coverage-quantile bound of $q=0.95$, $\alpha =1.05$ (right). Top row: the diagram's own points against the calibrated birth-persistence box, with the dashed square marking the region zoomed into below; the naive box is stretched by a single high-offspring outlier diagram elsewhere in the population, not shown. Bottom row: the same physical region of birth-persistence space, rendered at each calibration's native pixel density -- a $7\times 7$ block of mostly-fused pixels under the naive bound against a $16\times 16$ grid that keeps the secondary peak resolved under the quantile bound, since the same $64\times 64$ grid is stretched over a wider range}{figure.3}{}}
